def encode(message):
    """
    Write a function that takes a message, and encodes in such a 
    way that it swaps case of all letters, replaces all vowels in 
    the message with the letter that appears 2 places ahead of that 
    vowel in the english alphabet. 
    Assume only letters. 
    
    Examples:
    >>> encode('test')
    'TGST'
    >>> encode('This is a message')
    'tHKS KS C MGSSCGG'
    """
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""py2mpy — a *pure* CPython-AST -> K-constructor transliterator.

Reads a .py file and prints the matching `.mpy` term: a Lisp-indented tree of K
constructors that a (future) K semantics will parse. The design rule is strict:

    PURE TRANSLITERATION. Every Python AST node maps to exactly one K
    constructor with the same structure. The front-end makes NO semantic
    decisions — it does not desugar, simplify, normalize, or "interpret"
    anything. All meaning lives in the K semantics that consumes the term.

Consequences of that rule (vs. a "helpful" front-end):

  * `is` / `is not` transliterate to `CmpOp("is", ...)` / `CmpOp("is not", ...)`
    — NOT to `==` / `!=`. K decides what identity means.
  * a chained comparison `a < b < c` stays a single `Compare` carrying the
    chain (`Compare(a, CmpOp("<", b), CmpOp("<", c))`) — it is NOT pre-expanded
    into `a < b and b < c`.
  * a method call `x.append(v)` stays `Call(Attribute(Name("x"), "append"), v)`
    — there is no special `MethodCall` constructor folding the receiver in.
  * `a[i] = v` stays `Assign(Subscript(Name("a"), Name("i")), v)` — the target
    is just an expression in store position; no special `SubscriptAssign`.

The only choices the transliterator makes are unavoidably *syntactic* — e.g. a

OUTPUT TAIL
+                 | ordValue(KItem) [function]
+                 | chrValue(KItem) [function]
+                 | swapCaseValue(KItem) [function]
+
+  rule eval(Name(X), RHO) => RHO [ X ]
+  rule (_RHO:Map [ X <- V ]) [ X ] => V [simplification]
+  rule eval(Str(S), _RHO) => pyStr(S)
+  rule eval(Int(I), _RHO) => pyInt(I)
+  rule eval(BinOp("+", E1, E2), RHO)
+    => plusValue(eval(E1, RHO), eval(E2, RHO))
+  rule eval(Compare(E1, CmpOp("in", E2)), RHO)
+    => inValue(eval(E1, RHO), eval(E2, RHO))
+  rule eval(Call(Name("ord"), E:Expr), RHO) => ordValue(eval(E, RHO))
+  rule eval(Call(Name("chr"), E:Expr), RHO) => chrValue(eval(E, RHO))
+  rule eval(Call(Attribute(E, "swapcase"), .Exprs), RHO)
+    => swapCaseValue(eval(E, RHO))
+
+  rule plusValue(pyInt(I1), pyInt(I2)) => pyInt(I1 +Int I2)
+  rule plusValue(pyStr(S1), pyStr(S2)) => pyStr(S1 +String S2)
+  rule inValue(pyStr(NEEDLE), pyStr(HAYSTACK))
+    => pyBool(findString(HAYSTACK, NEEDLE, 0) >=Int 0)
+  rule ordValue(pyStr(C)) => pyInt(ordChar(C))
+  rule chrValue(pyInt(I)) => pyStr(chrChar(I))
+  rule swapCaseValue(pyStr(C)) => pyStr(swapCaseChar(C))
+
+  syntax String ::= swapCaseChar(String) [function, total]
+
+  syntax String ::= encodedChar(String) [function, total]
+                  | advancedVowel(String) [function, total]
+endmodule
